WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:04.140
- Three, two, one!
(hands clapping)

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.000
- [Narrator] A spacecraft
intentionally crashed

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.124
into an asteroid on September 26th.

00:00:08.124 --> 00:00:08.957
- Oh, wow.
(scientist laughs)

00:00:08.957 --> 00:00:10.260
(group applauds)

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.900
- [Narrator] As part of a
roughly $325 million mission.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.350
(group applauds)
- [Scientist] All right!

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:17.310
- [Narrator] The collision was part

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:19.830
of NASA's Double Asteroid
Redirection Test,

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.390
also known as DART.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.520
- This is a first for humankind.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:24.840
- [Narrator] So why did NASA crash

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.690
a multimillion dollar
spacecraft into an asteroid

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:29.640
and what will scientists learn from it?

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.970
There are currently over
one million known asteroids,

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:34.170
according to NASA.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:36.750
They vary in size, shape, and composition

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:38.880
and move in different
orbits around the sun.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.340
Some even cross paths with Earth's orbit,

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.860
but typically don't pose
a threat to our planet.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.420
But asteroid impacts have occurred

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:46.860
throughout Earth's history.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.900
Scientists believe an asteroid impact

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:50.820
led to the extinction of dinosaurs.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.860
In order to test one type of technology

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.560
that can mitigate a possible
future asteroid impact,

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.060
NASA launched DART.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.318
- [Scientist] Three, two, one.

00:00:59.318 --> 00:01:01.846
(rocket flare roars)

00:01:01.846 --> 00:01:05.850
And lift-off of the Falcon 9 and DART.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:06.960
- [Narrator] The DART spacecraft

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.020
launched into outer space
almost 10 months ago.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.730
The school bus-sized spacecraft

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:13.830
traveled millions of miles through space

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.660
and eventually collided with an asteroid

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.180
about the size of the Washington Monument.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:21.780
This is NASA's first ever
planetary defense test mission.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.100
- Planetary defense in general

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.370
is aimed at finding out what's out there,

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:28.110
figuring out if anything is out there

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:28.950
that we need to worry about,

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.300
and when we need to worry about it.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.200
- [Narrator] Andy Rivkin,

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.240
an investigation lead on the DART team,

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.520
identifies asteroids and develops methods

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.160
to mitigate the risk they
pose to planet Earth.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.110
He says this real-world experiment

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:41.520
will prepare defense methods

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.770
against future hazardous asteroids.

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:46.890
- It's the first time
that we are rearranging

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:49.140
a celestial body's orbit.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:49.973
- [Narrator] DART targeted

00:01:49.973 --> 00:01:51.990
the binary asteroid system Didymos,

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.390
which is composed of two asteroids,

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.160
Didymos, the larger asteroid,

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.620
and its smaller moonlet, Dimorphos.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:02.010
This is what scientists call
kinetic impactor technology,

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:03.720
which involves using spacecraft

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:05.880
designed to deflect a near-Earth object

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.330
to change its orbit so
that it misses our planet.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:10.830
According to NASA,

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.300
DART collided into the asteroid

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.700
at roughly 14,000 miles per hour

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:15.690
and the impact occurred

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:18.240
around seven million
miles away from Earth.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.040
But navigating a small spacecraft

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.660
leaves little room for error.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.040
- You can't sit there with a joystick

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.360
and steer yourself in that fast.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.670
- [Narrator] This is why
NASA created SMART Nav,

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.160
a new technology system
that allows the spacecraft

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.290
to direct itself entirely on its own,

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.420
without human intervention.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.760
Little was known about the
composition of Dimorphos

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:37.050
before the collision;

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:39.660
only pixelated telescopic and radar images

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:42.570
have provided small
clues about its surface.

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:45.780
But now scientists will be able
to study images of the crash

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:49.560
which were captured by an
Italian CubeSat called LICIACube.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.390
The images will help scientists determine

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:53.670
what kind of impact DART had on Dimorphos,

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:57.450
including a potential crater
on the surface of the asteroid.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.100
Scientists are also researching

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.710
how the impact changes
the asteroid's orbit.

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:03.510
Before impact, it took Dimorphos

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:07.290
approximately 11 hours and
54 minutes to orbit Didymos,

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:09.330
but that is likely to change now.

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:10.740
- We don't know if it will change it

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.830
to more like 11 hours and 42 minutes

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:16.380
or 11 hours and 46 minutes.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:18.660
- [Narrator] In order to
measure a change to the orbit,

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:20.580
Rivkin and his team will collect data

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:22.320
on the asteroid system's light curves,

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.690
which are measurements of a
celestial body's brightness

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:26.310
at certain intervals.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:27.420
Using the light curves,

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:29.010
scientists will be able to compare

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:30.870
the new orbit to the old orbit,

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:34.200
showing how much impact DART
made during the collision.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:35.760
In October 2024,

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:39.090
the European Space Agency will
launch a mission called Hera

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:41.250
which will reach Dimorphos in 2026

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:44.580
to conduct detailed post-impact
surveys on the asteroid.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.680
Hera will advance
scientists' understanding

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:48.660
of Dimorphos' internal properties

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:50.820
and the outcome of the DART test.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:52.230
Though DART is only a test

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.660
and Dimorphos isn't a
threat to planet Earth,

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:57.390
carrying out a real-world
test will inform scientists

00:03:57.390 --> 00:03:59.340
whether this type of planetary defense

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:01.740
is a reliable option
against future asteroids

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:02.700
headed towards Earth.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.160
- We don't wanna add
to people's anxieties,

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:07.200
we want to demonstrate

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.488
that we can handle asteroid deflections.

00:04:10.488 --> 00:04:13.071
(bright music)

